// `compiler/tyname.vl` — THE TYPE-NAME GRAMMAR, AND NOTHING ELSE.
//
// A rendered type NAME is a little bracket language the parser writes and five later
// passes read back: `{x:i32}` inline shape, `{[K]:V}` map, `T[]` array, `(…)` group and
// param list, `Head<A,B>` generic application, `"a"` quoted literal member, the
// separators `|` `&` `,` `:` and the function arrow `=>`. Every question anyone asks OF
// that language — does it open here, does it span the whole name, where does this group
// close, what is the element / key / value — lives in this file, and only here.
//
// **THIS MODULE IS A LEAF: IT HAS ZERO IMPORTS, AND THAT IS ITS WHOLE CONTRACT.** Every
// body below is a pure function of a STRING. No `T.` type arena, no `P.` node arena, no
// diagnostics, no module-level mutable state, no `emit_*`. Nothing here can observe the
// program being compiled, so nothing here can be reached by an import cycle — which is
// exactly why the family can finally live in one place.
//
// ── WHY THE HOME MOVED HERE, AND WHY IT COULD NOT BEFORE ─────────────────────────────
// These grammars were carved out in `emit_base.vl` (#1190, #1222, D-ARRELEM, D-SHAPEOPEN
// …), which is where the emitter needed them. The CHECKER asks the same questions and
// `emit_base.vl` imports `typecheck.vl`, so the edge is one-way and no copy in the
// checker could ever call a home up there: the checker hand-wrote 22 of its own instead.
// D-PARENDOWN / D-ARRDOWN / D-SHAPEDOWN / D-BRACEDOWN / D-GENAPPDOWN / D-MAPOPENDOWN
// answered that by INVERTING the home's location — the homes moved DOWN into
// `typecheck.vl`, the lowest module both sides could reach, and `emit_base.vl`
// republished the names so no emitter call site changed.
//
// "A name-grammar predicate in the CHECKER" was always the wrong layering read alone,
// and eight headers said so. The record filed a new shared module as the family's
// endgame with a precondition — one slice owning `typecheck.vl` plus six emitter modules
// at once — and #1228 REFUTED it by measurement: the bodies are pure, so the module is a
// LEAF, so `typecheck.vl` could re-export it exactly as `emit_base.vl` re-exported eleven
// of these names.
//
// **THAT RE-EXPORT CHAIN IS GONE, AND THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO DRAW IT AS IF IT WERE STILL
// THERE.** D-TYNAMEHOME phases 1a-1c re-pointed every consumer at this file and #1236
// deleted BOTH republishing lines (`typecheck.vl`'s and `emit_base.vl`'s), so the "up to
// THREE re-export hops" this header described is not a cost anyone pays any more — it is
// not a path that exists. **Zero hops. TEN direct importers, each exactly one:**
//
//     tyname (leaf, 0 imports)
//        ↑── typecheck · emit_base · emit_classify · emit_collect · emit_mono
//        ↑── emit_rep · emit_rewrite · emit_sections · driver · wasmEmit
//
// **THE TENTH IS NEW, AND THE PARAGRAPH IT REPLACES WAS THIS FILE'S OWN OPEN ITEM.** From
// phase 1 until W3 this header read "nine importers" and then warned that nine is the
// IMPORT set and not the set that NEEDS the grammar, naming `wasmEmit.vl` — which does
// type-name character work in `emitIs` / `emitUnionLitIs` — as a consumer that did not
// import the leaf. W3 took the edge: a zero-import leaf is reachable from everywhere, so
// it cannot cycle, and the missing consumer really was a decision no one had taken rather
// than a layering problem. The warning stays useful in its general form: an import set is
// not a needs set, and the next census should re-derive the consumers from what the code
// DOES rather than from what it imports.
//
// ── WHAT IS IN, AND WHAT IS DELIBERATELY OUT ─────────────────────────────────────────
// IN — 32 bodies, in eleven families, in dependency order:
//   the WALK      `skipQuotedName` (private) · `nameIsQuotedLeaf` · `tyGtIsClose` ·
//                 `tyTopIndexOf` · `tyGroupEndIndex`
//   SPACE RULE    `nameIsSpaceFree` (assert) · `nameStripSpaces` (enforce)
//   IDENT RUNS    `nameIdentChar` — the CLASS; the run TOKENIZER is measured and declined
//   GROUP INNER   `groupInnerOf` — the ONE cut every delimiter shares
//   PAREN         `nameIsFuncTypeAtom` · `parenEnclosesWhole` · `nameIsParenOpen` ·
//                 `nameIsParenSpanEnds` · `peelGroupParens`
//   FN ARROW      `fnParamsTextOf` · `fnRetTextOf` — the CUT; the INDEX stays out (below)
//   ARRAY         `nameIsArray` · `arrElemNameRaw` · `listElemNameOf`
//   BRACE         `nameIsShapeOpen` · `nameIsBraceSpanEnds`
//   FIELD COLON   `fieldNameOf` · `fieldTypeTextOf` — the CUT; the FINDER stays out (two
//                 different finders serve it, `indexOf(":")` and `tyTopIndexOf`)
//   MAP           `nameIsMapOpen` · `nameIsMapSpanEnds` · `mapSpellKeyEnd` ·
//                 `mapSpellKeyName` · `mapSpellValName`
//   GENERIC APP   `gaeLtAt` · `annGenAppSpanEnds` · `gaeHeadNameOf` · `gaeArgsTextOf`
//
// OUT, with the reason each time — the boundary is "pure function of a string", tested
// rather than eyeballed:
//   * **`isTopLevelFuncTypeName` STAYS IN `typecheck.vl`.** It is a string predicate that
//     also WRITES a module-level bank (`funcArrowAt`, D-ARROWBANK) which two call sites
//     read immediately after it returns. A body that reaches module state is not a leaf,
//     and publishing the bank through an accessor would put a CALL between the
//     predicate's return and the read — the one thing its own contract asks callers not
//     to do. Its private helper `topLevelArrowIndex` stays with it. **Its CUT does not
//     stay**: W4 brought `fnParamsTextOf` / `fnRetTextOf` here, taking the arrow index as
//     an ARGUMENT — precisely the half of that grammar that IS a pure function of a
//     string. The FINDER and the CUT are two things, and only one of them is stuck.
//   * **`splitUnionAtoms` / `unionMemberCount` STAY.** They are pure and cross-module,
//     and they are the obvious phase-2 candidates — but they are SPLITTERS that
//     materialise atoms, not endpoint tests / spans / cuts, and no D-* slice ever homed
//     them as part of this family. Moving them here would also drag the compiler's
//     hottest name function (~168 K corpus invocations) across a module boundary in a
//     slice whose claim is byte-identity.
//   * **`nameHasSep` / `nameHasPipe` STAY.** Pure, but private to the checker with no
//     consumer anywhere else: moving them buys a name, not a deduplication.
//   * **`emit_base.mapValNameOf` and `emit_base.nameIsShapeSpanEnds` STAY WHERE THEY
//     ARE.** They are LAYERS on these homes, not copies of them (the first has a
//     different SPAN rule and returns a deliberately garbled tail its callers strip
//     around), and `emit_base.vl` is outside this slice's partition.
//
// **THE FULL HEADER FOR EACH GRAMMAR IS BELOW, AT ITS BODY, VERBATIM FROM ITS HOME.**
// Nothing in this file is new logic: this slice is a MOVE, and the frozen-source rebuild
// is byte-identical to master's own output. Two present-tense claims in the walk's
// section header were measurably false and are corrected in place, marked where they sit.

// Skip a QUOTED string-literal segment inside a synthetic type name (the
// cursor is ON the opening `"`): returns the index just past the closing
// quote. A `\` escape skips the next char, so an escaped quote stays inside.
// Every depth-aware name scanner calls this so a literal member's characters
// (`"a|b"`) never count as separators or depth.
function skipQuotedName(name: string, start: i32) {
  let i = start + 1
  while i < name.length {
    const c = name[i]
    if c == '\\' { i = i + 2 }
    else if c == '"' { return i + 1 }
    else { i = i + 1 }
  }
  i
}

// ── the QUOTED-LEAF test: the OTHER half of the `"` alphabet ───────────────────────────
//
// THE ONE HOME of "is this type-name atom a string-literal member" — `"a"` of `"a"|"b"`,
// the `is` RHS of `v is "a"`, one member of an inline literal union. `skipQuotedName`
// above owns where a quoted segment ENDS; this owns where one BEGINS, and until here the
// second half was written out nine times while the first had a home.
//
// Re-censused structurally over the `'"'` character literal, index-0 spellings only, with
// both polarities: **9 writings in 8 functions** across FOUR modules —
// `emit_base.isLitVariantName` / `nulLitUnionInnerName` · `emit_classify.exprHasStrOp` ·
// `typecheck.litMemberTy` / `nameIsInlineLitUnion` (x2) / `nulElemListAtomKind` ·
// `wasmEmit.emitIs` / `emitUnionLitIs`. The census listed 8 and named
// `driver.modTypeRenamed`, which is NOT this test (a mid-scan `c == '"'` inside the
// identifier-run walk, W8's); it missed `emit_classify.exprHasStrOp` and
// `typecheck.nulElemListAtomKind` instead.
//
// **`wasmEmit.vl` IS THE TENTH CONSUMER AND THIS SLICE ADDS ITS IMPORT.** The header above
// has recorded since phase 1 that this file's nine importers are the import set and not the
// set that needs the grammar; two of the nine writings are `wasmEmit`'s, and a zero-import
// leaf is reachable from everywhere, so the edge cannot cycle. It is the first new
// consumer since the re-point, and the reason "nine consumers, all one hop" was never the
// same claim as "every module that needs the grammar has it".
//
// **NO LENGTH GUARD, AND THAT IS THE FAITHFUL FORM.** VL string indexing TRAPS out of
// bounds (unlike `.slice`, which clamps — verified by running both), so a bound folded in
// here would not be a tidy-up: it would turn a trap into `false` at the two sites that do
// NOT carry one (`litMemberTy` — guarded one line up by `name.length == 0` — and
// `emitUnionLitIs`, which is reached only through `isLitVariantName`, which carries one).
// The other seven guard themselves, and they do not agree on the bound: four spell
// `length < 2` (an atom needs BOTH quotes), two spell `> 0` / `== 0`. A home that folded
// in any one of those would be wrong for the rest — the same delimiter-blind ruling
// `groupInnerOf` took at twenty-two sites, applied to a bound instead of a delimiter.
export function nameIsQuotedLeaf(name: string) { name[0] == '"' }

// ── the IDENTIFIER-RUN walk: the CLASSIFIER is the home, and the TOKENIZER is REFUTED ──
//
// A rendered type name is identifier RUNS separated by the punctuation the rest of this
// file names. Two bodies in `driver.vl` walk it that way — `modGenParams` (the type
// parameters folded into a declared generic name) and `modTypeRenamed` (the module-merge
// rename, which rewrites every identifier segment through the rename map). Both spell the
// character class out, identically, four lines each; that is the operation written twice,
// and it is the one that gets a home.
//
// **THE CENSUS ASKED FOR A RUN TOKENIZER (`nameIdentRuns(name, outStart, outEnd)`). IT WAS
// BUILT, ROUTED, AND MEASURED — AND IT IS DECLINED, WITH THE NUMBER.** Two things came out
// of building it:
//
//   1. **It has only ONE legal consumer.** `modTypeRenamed` must not rename inside a quoted
//      literal member and skips those with a NAIVE scan (copy to the next `"`, "no escapes
//      in type names"). This file's quote rule is `skipQuotedName`, which honours `\`, and
//      the two disagree on exactly one shape: a literal member carrying an escaped quote
//      (`type T = "a\"b"`, which the compiler accepts and runs — verified). The naive scan
//      stops at the escaped quote and renames the `b` after it; `skipQuotedName` does not.
//      A quote-BLIND tokenizer is wrong for that site in the other direction (it would
//      rename inside every quoted member), so the site cannot take the walk at all. Giving
//      this file a SECOND quote rule to preserve the naive one would put the leaf's whole
//      contract at risk, and adopting `skipQuotedName` there is a silent BEHAVIOUR change
//      no refactor should carry.
//   2. **It costs 686 BYTES to serve that one consumer.** Three compilers, same source
//      otherwise: 1,042,805 B before this family, 1,043,413 B with `modGenParams` routed to
//      the tokenizer (**+608**), 1,042,727 B with the classifier-only form this file ships
//      (**−78**) — so the tokenizer is 686 B larger than the alternative it was meant to
//      replace. Two materialised `i32[]` index columns and their push loops cost more than
//      the one accumulating run they remove. A single-writing home changes a body's
//      LOCATION, not the count; here it changes the size too, in the wrong direction.
//
// **`typecheck.nameNeedsCanon` IS NOT THIS FAMILY EITHER, and the census's grouping is
// refuted by a witness rather than by an opinion.** It scans characters, but its digit rule
// is "a digit at a MEMBER-START position" (index 0, or after `|` / `:` / `,`) — not "a
// digit that starts an identifier run". `Box<0>` separates them: the digit starts a run and
// its predecessor `<` is not a member separator, so a run tokenizer answers TRUE where the
// site answers false, widening which names the canon pass rewrites.
//
// So the census's 5 sites are: 2 that share the CLASSIFIER (routed here), 1 that cannot
// take the walk (measured), and a fifth that is a different predicate (refuted).
//
// The identifier character class of a rendered type name: `[A-Za-z0-9_]`. Digits are IN (a
// run is `i32`, `f64`, `K0`), which is what makes this a different set from the LEXER's
// `isIdentStart`.
export function nameIdentChar(c: i32) {
  if c >= 'a' && c <= 'z' { return true }
  if c >= 'A' && c <= 'Z' { return true }
  if c >= '0' && c <= '9' { return true }
  c == '_'
}

// ── the SPACE-FREE NORMALISATION: one rule, ASSERTED in one place and ENFORCED in two ──
//
// "A CANONICAL type name has no spaces" is a rule the compiler states six times and had no
// name for. It matters because the two renderers disagree: `tyToStr` renders a shape SPACED
// (`{a: i32, b: string}`) for DIAGNOSTICS and `tyToEmitName` renders it tight (`{a:i32}`)
// for the `sNames` intern keys — so a space is the tell that a name came from the wrong
// renderer, and every field-set resolver has to read both as the same shape.
//
// Re-censused structurally over the `' '` character literal and over `strContains(x, " ")`
// (a spelling no character-level scan sees, and the census did not): **6 bodies, 8
// character operations** —
//   ASSERT   `emit_base.canonBareShapeName` · `emit_rewrite.synthRetAnnots`
//            (`!strContains(ctx, " ")` — same predicate, different spelling; ROUTED)
//   ENFORCE  `emit_classify.spacelessName` · `emit_rep.renderFaithful`
//   NEITHER  `emit_base.normTypeAtom` (a two-sided TRIM whose alphabet is `' '` AND `'\t'`)
//            `emit_classify.mvValKindOfName` (SKIP a run of spaces at an index, then slice
//            from it — a third operation, one writing, and stripping instead would also
//            drop the result's INTERIOR spaces)
// Two of the six therefore stay, with their reason. **THE FOURTH ASSERTION/ENFORCEMENT SITE
// IS NOW ROUTED TOO** — `emit_rewrite.synthRetAnnots`, filed by #1256 as outside its file
// partition, is in this cycle's. Equal on every input rather than on every reachable one: with
// a one-character needle `strContains` rejects the empty string on its `sub.length > s.length`
// line — which, negated, is the `true` this home answers — and otherwise walks the identical
// `s[i] == ' '` compare. **The routable share of this rule is closed at 4 of 6, with the two
// declines measured rather than deferred.**
//
// THE ONE HOME of the ASSERTION: does `name` contain no space at all.
export function nameIsSpaceFree(name: string) {
  let i = 0
  while i < name.length {
    if name[i] == ' ' { return false }
    i = i + 1
  }
  true
}
// THE ONE HOME of the ENFORCEMENT: `name` with every space dropped.
//
// **IT RETURNS `name` ITSELF WHEN THERE IS NOTHING TO DROP, AND THAT IS MEASURED RATHER
// THAN DEFENSIVE.** `spacelessName`'s own header counted 18,666 of 89,166 shape names
// carrying a space (21%) over corpus + fuzz: the normalisation is LIVE, and the other 79%
// must not pay an allocation for it. So the scan runs first and the rebuild starts from
// `name.slice(0, i)` at the FIRST space — the copies' shape, kept exactly.
//
// It is NOT a depth ladder: it counts no brackets and asks nothing about structure, so it
// reintroduces none of the grammar this file's other bodies own.
export function nameStripSpaces(name: string) {
  let i = 0
  while i < name.length {
    if name[i] == ' ' {
      let out = name.slice(0, i)
      let j = i
      while j < name.length {
        if name[j] != ' ' { out = out + fromCodePoint(name[j]) }
        j = j + 1
      }
      return out
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  name
}

// `>` at index `i` is a generic-application CLOSER exactly when an earlier `<` is still
// OPEN (`angOpen` — the caller's running count of unclosed `<`) and it is not the second
// character of the function arrow `=>`.
//
// WHERE `<`/`>` IS A BRACKET, AND WHERE IT PROVABLY IS NOT. The only producer of a `<` in
// any string these scanners see is `parseTypeAtom`'s generic-APPLICATION arm
// (`name + "<" + parseTypeName()`, `compiler/parser.vl`), whose own comment records why the
// token is unambiguous there — "in TYPE position the `<` is unambiguous (no comparison can
// appear here)". A parser-built type name never contains an EXPRESSION, so the RELATIONAL
// `<`/`>` cannot reach here.
//
// A bare `>` is a different matter, and the `angOpen` gate is there because MEASUREMENT
// refuted the simpler "a `>` always closes" rule. These scanners are shared by three
// string languages, and two of them spell a `>` that closes nothing:
//   * `tyToStr`'s DIAGNOSTIC render spells the arrow `->` (`() -> i64 | f64`);
//   * the `$fnsig` closure-signature KEY spells the param/return split `>`
//     (`"=>ii>i"` — `emit_base.annArrowAt`, `emit_mono`).
// Over the 1,274-file corpus an audit probe counted **657** `<` against **927** bare `>`,
// with **263** inputs driven to a NEGATIVE depth by exactly those two spellings — so a
// rule keyed on the character alone is wrong, and a `>` that closes nothing must stay
// inert. `angOpen > 0` is the total condition: no `<` open, no closer.
//
// The `=` exemption sits INSIDE the gate, for the one case where both hold — an arrow
// nested in a generic argument, `Box<(i32)=>i32>`. (Counting that `>` would close the
// application one bracket early.)
export function tyGtIsClose(name: string, i: i32, angOpen: i32) {
  if angOpen <= 0 { return false }
  if i > 0 { return name[i - 1] != '=' }
  true
}

// ── THE ONE TYPE-NAME SCANNER ────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A rendered type name is a bracket language: `{}` object / map, `[]` array,
// `()` group and param list, `<>` generic application, `"…"` literal member, plus
// the separators `|` `&` `,` `:` and the function arrow `=>`. Walking it at TOP
// LEVEL — the same `depth`/`angOpen`/quote-skip ladder — was hand-written out
// FOURTEEN times in this file alone, and eleven more times across `emit_base`,
// `emit_collect` and `emit_classify`.
//
// That is not a style problem, it is the compiler's most-repeated BUG SHAPE: every
// extension of the grammar has had to be re-taught to each copy, and the copies
// have twice been caught disagreeing — #1118/#1120 taught `<…>` to the scanners
// that had never heard of it, #1140 collapsed three copies of the ref-array
// ladder. FOUR DIVERGENCES WERE NAMED HERE AND ALL FOUR ARE NOW CLOSED — recorded
// as history rather than as a live warning, because the next census must not
// re-derive them: `parenEnclosesWhole` and `nameIsFuncTypeAtom` ride the home below
// and so skip quoted members; `splitUnionAtoms` and `unionMemberCount` lost the naked
// `inStr` toggle an escaped quote (`"a\"b"`) walked straight through, and ask
// `tyTopIndexOf` instead (the token `inStr` no longer occurs in the tree outside this
// sentence); and `canonShapeName`'s map-key scan is `mapSpellKeyEnd`, which is
// `tyGroupEndIndex`. One home ended the class.
//
// The two entry points below are the WHOLE grammar. Everything else that counts a
// bracket asks them a question; nothing else counts one.
//
// **THE "ELEVEN MORE TIMES" ABOVE IS DISCHARGED, AND SAYING SO IS THE POINT** — a
// stale hand-off is what inflates the next census. It was true when this home was
// minted and has been false since D-SHAPEFIELD/D-PARAMARR closed the last copy.
// Measured structurally over `angOpen`, the ladder's one unmistakable marker: **14
// occurrences in this module and ZERO in executable code anywhere else in the tree**
// (`emit_base` 1, `emit_classify` 2 — all three inside comments that name this home).
// No module imports `tyGtIsClose` today either; it stays exported because the close
// rule is part of the two entry points' contract, not because a copy needs it.
// The per-consumer diffs are in `docs/internals/destringify-types-program.md`.

// The index of the first TOP-LEVEL occurrence of `a` — or of `b`, when `b` is not
// 0 — at or after `from`, or -1. Quoted literal members skip whole (escape-aware,
// via `skipQuotedName`); `{}`/`[]`/`()`/`<>` nest; a `>` closes only against an
// OPEN `<` (`tyGtIsClose`), so the arrow's own `>` and `tyToStr`'s `->` stay inert.
//
// `'='` means the function ARROW: it matches only when the next character is `>`.
// The four callers that hand-rolled that two-character lookahead now share it.
//
// DEPTH IS COUNTED FROM `from`, and that is exact rather than approximate: a
// caller resuming a split at the character after a top-level separator resumes at
// a point where the depth was 0 by the split's own condition, so restarting the
// count there reproduces it. A whole split therefore still costs ONE pass over the
// name — the scan runs from the previous separator to the next, not from 0 each
// time — which is what keeps `splitUnionAtoms` (the hottest caller at ~168 K
// invocations over the corpus) off the O(n²) path its own header warns about.
export function tyTopIndexOf(name: string, a: i32, b: i32, from: i32) {
  let depth = 0
  let angOpen = 0
  let i = from
  while i < name.length {
    const c = name[i]
    if c == '"' {
      i = skipQuotedName(name, i)
    } else {
      if c == '{' || c == '[' || c == '(' {
        depth = depth + 1
      } else if c == '<' {
        depth = depth + 1
        angOpen = angOpen + 1
      } else if c == '}' || c == ']' || c == ')' {
        depth = depth - 1
      } else if c == '>' && tyGtIsClose(name, i, angOpen) {
        depth = depth - 1
        angOpen = angOpen - 1
      } else if depth == 0 && (c == a || c == b) {
        if c != '=' { return i }
        if i + 1 < name.length && name[i + 1] == '>' { return i }
      }
      i = i + 1
    }
  }
  -1
}

// The index at which the group opened by `name[from - 1]` CLOSES — the first
// closer that would take the depth (counted from `from`) below zero — or -1 when
// the group never closes. The same ladder as `tyTopIndexOf`, with the closer as
// the target instead of a separator.
//
// Callers gate on the OPENER themselves (`name[0] == '('`, `inner[0] == '['`),
// which is what makes `from` meaningful; a `>` cannot be the closer found here
// because `tyGtIsClose` needs an open `<` that a `from`-relative depth of 0 has
// not seen.
export function tyGroupEndIndex(name: string, from: i32) {
  let depth = 0
  let angOpen = 0
  let i = from
  while i < name.length {
    const c = name[i]
    if c == '"' {
      i = skipQuotedName(name, i)
    } else {
      if c == '{' || c == '[' || c == '(' {
        depth = depth + 1
      } else if c == '<' {
        depth = depth + 1
        angOpen = angOpen + 1
      } else if c == '}' || c == ']' || c == ')' {
        if depth == 0 { return i }
        depth = depth - 1
      } else if c == '>' && tyGtIsClose(name, i, angOpen) {
        if depth == 0 { return i }
        depth = depth - 1
        angOpen = angOpen - 1
      }
      i = i + 1
    }
  }
  -1
}

// ── the GROUP-INTERIOR cut: ONE cut, and it is deliberately DELIMITER-BLIND ────────────
//
// THE ONE HOME of the group-interior peel: `name` minus its FIRST and LAST characters —
// the body of a one-character-delimited group. `{x:i32}` → `x:i32`, `(i32|null)` →
// `i32|null`, `"a"` → `a`. `""` for a name too short to have an interior.
//
// **THIS IS THE "TEST HALF HOMED, CUT HALF NOT" SHAPE, FOR THE THIRD TIME.** The array
// grammar had it (D-ARRELEM: `nameIsArray` was homed, the `[]` cut was written out nine
// times), the map grammar had it (D-MAPSPELL), and this file already owns every SPAN TEST
// of the group family — `nameIsParenSpanEnds`, `nameIsBraceSpanEnds`, `nameIsMapSpanEnds`
// — and, until this line, no interior CUT at all. Censused structurally over
// comment-stripped source, `X.slice(1, X.length - 1)` is written out **18 times tree-wide**
// (`emit_base` 3, `emit_classify` 10, `emit_collect` 2, `typecheck` 4 — including this
// file's own `peelGroupParens` and `listElemNameOf`, which are routed below), which makes
// it the largest single duplicated operation in the compiler's type-name work.
//
// **IT CARRIES NO ENDPOINT TEST, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN, NOT AN OMISSION.** The array cut
// could fold its test in (`arrElemNameRaw` gates on `nameIsArray`) because the array
// grammar has exactly ONE test. This cut serves THREE delimiters and the routed sites do
// not agree on which one they want in front of it: three ask `nameIsParenSpanEnds`, four
// ask `nameIsShapeSpanEnds`, one asks the bare `nameIsShapeOpen` — and three are gated by
// their CALLER instead. A cut that folded in any one of those would be wrong for the other
// two, and a cut that folded in the DISJUNCTION would be wider than every site. So the
// test stays at the call site, exactly as `nameIsParenSpanEnds` carries no balance walk
// for the same reason (its header records the six sites such a widening would have
// tightened).
//
// **THE `< 2` BOUND IS NOT A NARROWING — IT IS THE SLICE'S OWN ANSWER, MEASURED.** VL's
// `.slice` clamps rather than traps, so `"{".slice(1, 0)` and `"".slice(1, -1)` both
// evaluate to `""` (verified by running both). This home therefore agrees with the raw
// expression on EVERY input, not merely on every input reachable at the routed sites —
// which is what lets the routing be justified by the operation instead of by eighteen
// separate reachability arguments. The bound is kept anyway: it states the contract, and
// it is the same `length >= 2` all three span tests above already spell.
//
// (The cut is BOUND rather than left as the trailing expression: a bare member-call
// statement is not a lowerable form — `emitProgram: unsupported member-call statement` —
// the same reason `arrElemNameRaw` binds its slice.)
export function groupInnerOf(name: string) {
  if name.length < 2 { return "" }
  const inner = name.slice(1, name.length - 1)
  inner
}

// True if the type-name `name` is a FUNCTION type (`() => i64`, `(i32) => i32`) — a
// TOP-LEVEL `=>` (parens/braces/brackets balanced). A value-union member that is a
// closure rides the box's anyref `value` as the closure fat-pointer struct (a
// `structref` subtype), exactly as a string/list member rides its wrapper ref — so it
// is a REF atom (kind 11), with no per-rep scalar value box. Local to typecheck (the
// emitter's `annArrowAt` lives in `emit_base`, which imports THIS module).
export function nameIsFuncTypeAtom(nameIn: string) {
  // A union member keeps its GROUPING parens (`(() => i64) | i64` splits to the atom
  // `(() => i64)`), so peel every fully-enclosing balanced paren layer before the
  // top-level `=>` scan — otherwise the arrow reads as nested (depth 1) and misses.
  // THIS LOOP WAS THE HAND-WRITTEN COPY `peelGroupParens` was carved out of: character
  // for character the same span test, the same walk conjunct, the same slice. It could
  // not call the home while the home lived in `emit_base.vl` (which imports THIS file);
  // now the home lives below and this is one call. The `length >= 2` conjunct is IMPLIED
  // by the span test, not dropped — a one-character name cannot be both `(` and `)`.
  const name = peelGroupParens(nameIn)
  tyTopIndexOf(name, '=', 0, 0) >= 0
}

// True when the LEADING bracket of `name` closes only at the FINAL char — i.e. the outer
// group encloses the whole string (`(()=>i64)` yes; `(i32)=>(i32)` no, the first `)`
// closes mid-string). Lets `nameIsFuncTypeAtom` strip only genuine grouping parens.
// Both callers (`nameIsFuncTypeAtom`, `peelGroupParens`) gate on `name.length >= 2` and
// `name[0] == '('` before calling, so the `<…>` arm only ever runs on a group NESTED
// inside those parens; a bare `Box<i32>` reaching here would answer true, which is
// correct for "the leading bracket closes at the end" but is not a paren peel — do not
// drop those guards. The LENGTH guard is load-bearing too, and differently: on the EMPTY
// name this answers true (`tyGroupEndIndex` returns -1, and `"".length - 1` is also -1)
// where the hand-written scan it replaces answered false. No caller can reach it — both
// require two characters — but a third caller must gate, or add the guard here.
export function parenEnclosesWhole(name: string) {
  tyGroupEndIndex(name, 1) == name.length - 1
}

// ── the PAREN grammar: `nameIsParenOpen` OPENS it, `nameIsParenSpanEnds` SPANS it ──────
//
// THE HOMES LIVE HERE, ONE MODULE BELOW THE EMITTER, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT.
// They were carved out in `emit_base.vl` (#1190), which imports THIS module — so the
// checker, which hand-wrote the same three character compares at six sites of its own,
// could not reach them. Relocating them here (D-PARENDOWN) inverts the home's LOCATION
// without touching the import graph: `emit_base.vl` keeps exporting both names as
// one-line delegations, so every caller in `emit_collect`/`emit_mono` compiles untouched.
// The grammar exists ONCE, in this file. Nothing here is a second copy.
//
// A census of the `'('` / `')'` CHARACTER literals reads 47 tree-wide (27 open, 20 close),
// of which 4 are `lexer`/`format` source-TEXT scanners and 5 are this file's bracket-CLASS
// depth ladders (`c == '{' || c == '[' || c == '('` — membership in a walk, not a name-shape
// question). The remaining 38 over type names are SIX syntactic spellings, and — exactly as
// the SHAPE-NAME family turned out to be six spellings of THREE predicates — they reduce to
// **TWO**:
//
//   * **`nameIsParenOpen`** — "the name starts a paren group". Written out ALONE at 7 sites
//     (`emit_base.nameIsClosureArray`, `emit_collect` 3972, `emit_mono` 973,
//     `emit_classify` ×4), and as the FIRST HALF of every one of the other spellings.
//   * **`nameIsParenSpanEnds`** below — the same plus a final `)`, at 6 sites.
//
// THE OTHER FOUR SPELLINGS ARE NOT THIS PREDICATE AND MUST NOT BE ROUTED HERE. Three of
// them close the group somewhere that is NOT the last character:
//
//   * ARROW-relative (`name[arrowAt - 1] == ')'`) — `emit_base.annFnDecompose`,
//     `isTopLevelFuncTypeName` above, `nameToTyReal`'s function-type arm. The param list
//     closes before the `=>`, so the last character belongs to the RETURN.
//   * ARRAY-relative (`name[name.length - 3] == ')'`) — `emit_base.parenUnionArrElemName`.
//     The last two characters are the `[]`.
//   * CLOSE-ONLY (`canonEmitName`'s paren-result arm) — no opener test at all.
//
// Their OPEN half is still this predicate and is routed; their CLOSE test stays inline.
//
// **AND THE NAIVE HOME IS THE HOME FOR NONE OF THEM.** "A parenthesized type name" most
// obviously means the endpoints PLUS a balance walk proving the group opened at index 0 is
// still open at the last character — `parenEnclosesWhole` above does exactly that. Building
// THAT as the home and routing the span sites to it would have silently TIGHTENED 6 sites /
// 12 occurrences that carry no walk today (`emit_base.listElemNameOf`, `emit_classify`
// 4510 / 6363 / 10046, and this file's `nulElemListAtomKind` / `litUnionArrayElemOf`).
// So this home carries NO walk, precisely as `emit_base.nameIsShapeSpanEnds` carries none,
// and the two sites that DO walk keep their walk as a separate conjunct at the call site.
export function nameIsParenOpen(name: string) {
  if name.length < 1 { return false }
  name[0] == '('
}
// THE ONE HOME of the paren-group ENDPOINT span: `nameIsParenOpen` AND the final character
// is `)`. A LAYER over the opener, not a second copy of it — the `'('` compare lives once,
// above.
//
// The `length >= 2` bound five of the six routed sites spell (as `< 2` early returns, or as
// enclosing `length > 1` blocks one scope up) is IMPLIED, not dropped: on a one-character
// name `name[0]` and `name[name.length - 1]` are the SAME character, and no character is
// both `(` and `)`, so the explicit bound and this opener-gated form agree on every input.
// The sixth (`emit_classify.rlCanonLitUnionAtoms` 10046) bounds only `length >= 1` and is
// the same argument read the other way round.
export function nameIsParenSpanEnds(name: string) {
  if !nameIsParenOpen(name) { return false }
  name[name.length - 1] == ')'
}
// Peel every fully-enclosing balanced grouping-paren layer from `name` (`(() => i64)` →
// `() => i64`), returning a non-parenthesized name unchanged. `splitUnionAtoms` KEEPS a closure
// member's grouping parens (`(() => i64) | i32` → atom `(() => i64)`), but the bare `is`-test /
// annotation spelling drops them (`() => i64`) — so an atom-equality compare must peel first.
// (A ref-array / closure-array element ends in `]`, a struct in `}`, so only a BARE closure atom
// is affected; the paren-peel is the `nameIsFuncTypeAtom` prelude, shared to stay in lockstep.)
export function peelGroupParens(nameIn: string) {
  let name = nameIn
  // The PAREN grammar's SPAN half at its home, then the walk. `parenEnclosesWhole`'s header
  // requires both its callers to gate on `length >= 2` and a leading `(` before calling: the
  // home enforces both — it can only answer true when two distinct characters are `(` and
  // `)` — so the contract survives the routing intact. The walk stays a separate conjunct
  // because the OTHER walk-carrying site (`emit_base.normTypeAtom`) rides a DIFFERENT walk.
  // The GROUP-INTERIOR cut at its home (`groupInnerOf`) — the SPAN test above is the guard
  // this cut deliberately does not carry, so the pair reads exactly as it did inline.
  while nameIsParenSpanEnds(name) && parenEnclosesWhole(name) {
    name = groupInnerOf(name)
  }
  name
}

// ── the FUNCTION-ARROW CUT: the pair whose INDEX could not come with it ───────────────
//
// `(p1, p2) => ret` split into the PARAM-LIST interior and the RETURN, GIVEN the index of
// the top-level `=>`. Re-censused structurally over `.slice(1, <var> - 1)` and
// `.slice(<var> + 2, …)`: **8 writings in 5 functions** —
//   PARAM   `emit_base.annFnDecompose` · `typecheck.nameToTyReal` / `canonEmitNameAt`
//   RETURN  the same three, plus `emit_base.annRetNameOf` and
//           `emit_classify.shapeFieldTypeCompat`
// The census said 7 and never listed `typecheck.nameToTyReal`, which writes BOTH halves.
//
// **THE INDEX STAYS WHERE IT IS, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE SHAPE OF THIS PAIR.** The obvious
// route — point the checker at `emit_base.annArrowAt` — is an UP-move (`emit_base.vl`
// imports `typecheck.vl`), and the checker's own arrow FINDER, `isTopLevelFuncTypeName`,
// cannot move DOWN here either: it writes the module-level `funcArrowAt` bank
// (D-ARROWBANK) that two of its call sites read on the very next line, and a body that
// reaches module state is not a leaf (this file's OUT list has said so since phase 0). So
// the FINDERS stay in three places and only the CUT — pure, given the index — moves. Both
// routed checker sites hand in `funcArrowAt`; the two `emit_base` sites hand in
// `annArrowAt`'s return; `emit_classify` hands in its own. Nothing about the bank changes,
// and no call is inserted between a predicate's return and its bank read.
//
// **DELIMITER-BLIND, AND HERE THE GUARDS ARE FOUR DIFFERENT PREDICATES, NOT FOUR BOUNDS.**
// `annRetNameOf` gates `at < 1`; `annFnDecompose` gates `fa < 1` PLUS `nameIsParenOpen`
// PLUS an arrow-relative `ann[fa - 1] == ')'`; `nameToTyReal` and `canonEmitNameAt` gate on
// `isTopLevelFuncTypeName`, which is that conjunction one module down;
// `shapeFieldTypeCompat` gates `aa >= 0` — WIDER than every other site, and the one that
// proves the cut must fold nothing in. `.slice` clamps rather than traps, so its cut at
// `aa == 0` is a legal slice and the home agrees with it there, on that input as on every
// other.
//
// The PARAM-LIST interior of `(p1, p2) => ret` given the top-level `=>` at `at`: the text
// between the leading `(` and the `)` that closes at `at - 1`.
export function fnParamsTextOf(name: string, at: i32) {
  const ps = name.slice(1, at - 1)
  ps
}
// The RETURN of `(p1, p2) => ret` given the top-level `=>` at `at`: everything past the two
// arrow characters. (Bound rather than left as the trailing expression: a bare member-call
// statement is not a lowerable form, the same reason `emit_base.annRetNameOf` binds its own
// slice — which is now this call.)
export function fnRetTextOf(name: string, at: i32) {
  const ret = name.slice(at + 2, name.length)
  ret
}

// ── the ARRAY-NAME grammar: `nameIsArray` TESTS it, `arrElemNameRaw` CUTS it ───────────
//
// **BOTH HOMES MOVED DOWN HERE FROM `emit_base.vl` (D-ARRDOWN), for the same reason the
// PAREN pair above did:** they are pure character grammar over a type-name STRING, and
// living above the checker meant the checker re-wrote them. `nameToTyReal`, `canonEmitName`,
// `nulElemListAtomKind` and `litUnionArrayElemOf` each carried their own copy of the
// two-character suffix test, and three of them their own copy of the cut.
//
// True if the type-name `name` is an ARRAY type — its lexeme ends in `[]` (the suffix
// `parseType` appends for every `T[]` annotation).
export function nameIsArray(name: string) {
  const n = name.length
  if n < 2 { return false }
  if name[n - 2] != '[' { return false }
  if name[n - 1] != ']' { return false }
  true
}
// THE ONE HOME of the trailing-`[]` CUT: the RAW 1-D element name of an array type name
// — `name` minus its final two characters — or "" when `name` is not an array name.
//
// The grammar is one rule with two halves ("a name ending in `[]` is a 1-D list, and its
// element is that name minus those two characters"), and only the TEST half had a home.
// Censused over comment-stripped source at `3974381`, the CUT half was hand-written
// **nine** times in `emit_base.vl` alone and the TEST half **seven**, in ten functions.
//
// It is RAW deliberately — `listElemNameOf` below is this cut PLUS the naive
// grouping-paren strip, and the two are not interchangeable. `emit_mono.vl`'s own comment
// at its hand-written copy says exactly that ("The `[]` cut stays RAW here (not
// `listElemNameOf`): that home strips one …"), which is a caller asking for a home that
// did not exist.
//
// `""` doubles as "not an array name" and as the element of the degenerate `"[]"`, the
// convention `listElemNameOf` already documents and its four callers already read as
// `false`. The three sites where those two answers are NOT interchangeable
// (`emit_base.nameIsClosureArray`, `monoTyParamOf`, `monoBindFromAnn` — each of which does
// something other than decline on an empty element) keep an explicit `nameIsArray` test in
// front, so this merge asserts nothing about whether `"[]"` is a producible type name.
// That candidate WAS built and measured: sabotage S4 installs the one-call form at the two
// `mono*` sites and reddens 0 of 1,398 corpus files and 0 of 38,400 fuzz programs. A 0 is
// not agreement when the divergence population is empty — it is the absence of a witness,
// so the faithful two-call form ships and the one-call form is filed with its measurement.
// (The cut is BOUND rather than left as the trailing expression: a bare member-call
// statement is not a lowerable form — `emitProgram: unsupported member-call statement` —
// which is why `emit_base.annRetNameOf` binds its slice the same way.)
export function arrElemNameRaw(name: string) {
  if !nameIsArray(name) { return "" }
  const elem = name.slice(0, name.length - 2)
  elem
}
// THE ONE HOME of the 1-D LIST-ELEMENT peel: the trailing `[]` cut plus the ONE layer of
// disambiguating grouping parens a composite element carries (`(boolean|null)[]` →
// `boolean|null`, `K[]` → `K`). "" when `name` is not an array name.
//
// Four `nameIsX[]` classifiers in `emit_base.vl` wrote this same five-line peel out by hand
// (`nameIsNulBoolList` / `nameIsNulStrList` / `nameIsNulLitUnionList` /
// `nameIsLitUnionArray`) — each with its own `nameIsArray` gate and its own paren strip —
// and so did this file's `nulElemListAtomKind` and `litUnionArrayElemOf`, which could not
// reach the home until it moved here. Named once, the family cannot drift on what an
// element IS.
//
// The `[]` cut itself is `arrElemNameRaw`'s, above: this function is the RAW cut plus the
// paren strip, not a second copy of the cut. The paren strip is deliberately the NAIVE one
// the copies carried (`nameIsParenSpanEnds` — first char `(`, last char `)`, no balance
// walk), NOT `peelGroupParens`: every element reaching here is a single canonical atom, and
// widening the strip to a balanced one would change what `(a)|(b)`-shaped elements classify
// as. THIS IS THE SITE THE WALK-CARRYING HOME WOULD HAVE TIGHTENED, and the reason the span
// home above carries no walk. `""` doubles as "not an array" and as the element of the
// degenerate `"[]"` — every consumer answers `false` for both, exactly as the copies did.
//
// The nested `if`s this replaced put their length bound (`elem.length > 1`) one scope UP
// from the character compares; the home's bound is `>= 1`, and the two agree because a
// one-character name cannot be both `(` and `)` (see the span home's header).
export function listElemNameOf(name: string) {
  let elem = arrElemNameRaw(name)
  if elem == "" { return "" }
  if nameIsParenSpanEnds(elem) { elem = groupInnerOf(elem) }
  elem
}

// ── the BRACE grammar: `nameIsShapeOpen` OPENS a shape, `nameIsBraceSpanEnds` SPANS ANY ──
//
// **THE OPENER MOVED DOWN HERE FROM `emit_base.vl` (D-SHAPEDOWN);** the WIDE SPAN followed
// it (D-BRACEDOWN, below), and only the CONJUNCTION of the two (`nameIsShapeSpanEnds`) stays
// up there, because no site in this file asks for it. THE ONE HOME of the inline-object-shape
// OPENER: does `name` start an inline struct shape (`{x:i32}`) rather than a map shape
// (`{[string]:i32}`) — the `length >= 2 && name[0] == '{' && name[1] != '['` triple.
//
// Censused STRUCTURALLY over comment-stripped source (char bindings resolved — every real
// scanner binds the char first, so a detector anchored on `name[0] == '{'` alone reads
// almost nothing), the index-0 `'{'` test is written out **35** times tree-wide
// (`emit_classify` 15 · `emit_base` 7 · `typecheck` 6 · `emit_collect` 4 · `emit_rep` 2 ·
// `format` 1) and the index-1 `'['` test **27** times. **20** of those sites carry BOTH and
// so belong to this grammar.
//
// THE 20 ARE SIX SYNTACTIC SPELLINGS OF ONLY **THREE** PREDICATES, and that is the whole
// reason this needed measuring rather than eyeballing. Counted by form, including WHERE the
// length bound sits: positive same-line conjunction (8); the same conjunction with the bound
// hoisted to an ENCLOSING `if name.length >= 2 {` block (2); with the bound hoisted to an
// EARLY-RETURN guard (1); the fully De-Morganed guard `if nm.length < 2 || nm[0] != '{' ||
// nm[1] == '[' … { return … }` (6); the multi-line parenthesised conjunction (2); and one
// `length > 0` outlier. Reduced to predicates they are only:
//
//   * **this home** — `len >= 2 · OPEN · NOT2ND`;
//   * **`emit_base.nameIsShapeSpanEnds`** — the same plus a final `}`, at 10 sites;
//   * **`emit_classify.nameIsWholeSpanShape`** — the same plus `tyGroupWrapsWhole`, at 1.
//
// Only ONE of the 20 sites lacks `length >= 2`: `emit_collect.funcRetUnrepresentable` gates
// on `length > 0`, which on the single name `"{"` reads `nm[1]` OUT OF BOUNDS. VL string
// indexing **TRAPS** there (`wasm trap: out of bounds array access`, verified by hand), so
// that spelling is not a wider predicate — it is the same predicate plus a latent trap, and
// routing it here is equivalence-preserving on every input that does not already crash.
//
// **THIS FILE'S OTHER THREE `{`…`}` TESTS ARE NOT THIS PREDICATE** (`nameToTyReal`'s
// inline-object arm, `canonEmitName`'s shape arm, `isObjShapeName`): each is
// `name[0] == '{' && name[len - 1] == '}'` WITHOUT the `name[1] != '['` conjunct, so each
// ACCEPTS a map name, and routing them HERE would blind the checker to map-valued shapes.
// D-MAPSPELL left them alone for exactly that reason; they are the WIDE span's, and it is
// now directly below. The two that REQUIRE `name[1] == '['` — the complement, not this —
// are the MAP-NAME grammar's, further down.
export function nameIsShapeOpen(name: string) {
  if name.length < 2 { return false }
  name[0] == '{' && name[1] != '['
}
// THE ONE HOME of the WIDE BRACE ENDPOINT span: `length >= 2`, a `{` at index 0, and a `}` as
// the final character — WITHOUT the `name[1] != '['` conjunct, so it accepts a MAP name as
// well as a shape. Carved out in `emit_base.vl` by D-GRAMPRED (#1222) and **moved down here
// (D-BRACEDOWN) for the reason its own header predicted and #1224 then proved impossible to
// discharge from above**: that header offered the three copies in THIS file a route to
// `emit_base`, and `emit_base.vl` imports THIS module, so the route would have cycled the
// module graph. Inverting the home's LOCATION is what makes the route legal — the same move
// the PAREN / ARRAY / SHAPE-OPEN grammars already took. `emit_base.vl` keeps publishing the
// name (one `export … from` line), so `emit_classify`'s two callers compile untouched.
//
// The brace family is now four predicates over `length >= 2` names opening with `{`:
//
//   * `nameIsShapeOpen`             — OPEN · NOT2ND                        (above)
//   * `nameIsMapOpen`               — OPEN · 2ND       — the complement    (below)
//   * `nameIsBraceSpanEnds`         — OPEN · CLOSE     — this home
//   * `emit_base.nameIsShapeSpanEnds` — the first ANDed with this one
//
// **THIS IS NOT `nameIsShapeSpanEnds` AND NOT `emit_classify.nameIsWholeSpanShape`.** It is
// the WIDEST of them: it accepts `{[string]:i32}`, which the other two reject, and
// `{a:{b:i32}}|{c:i32}`, which `nameIsWholeSpanShape`'s balance walk rejects. Tightening a
// caller from this home to either of the others is a behaviour change owing its own graded
// population, not a refactor — and all three of this file's routed sites WANT the width:
// `nameToTyReal`'s arm is reached only after the map arm above it has declined, and
// `canonEmitName` / `isObjShapeName` both hand a map name on to a shape walk deliberately.
//
// #1222's header named FIVE copies outside its partition (`emit_classify` ×2, `typecheck`
// ×3). **The `emit_classify` two were routed by #1223** and the file no longer contains a
// `'}'` character literal at all; the three here are the whole remainder, and this move takes
// them. The predicate reaches ZERO hand-written copies tree-wide with this slice.
export function nameIsBraceSpanEnds(name: string) {
  if name.length < 2 { return false }
  name[0] == '{' && name[name.length - 1] == '}'
}

// ── the FIELD-COLON CUT: the shape BODY's per-field split, W14 ────────────────────────
//
// A `{…}` shape body is a comma-separated list of `name:type` fields, and every pass that
// reads one splits a field at its colon INDEX. The brace grammar above owns where the
// group opens and spans and `groupInnerOf` owns the interior peel; until here nothing
// owned the cut every one of their callers takes NEXT.
//
// Re-censused structurally — every `.slice(0, <ix>)` / `.slice(<ix> + 1, <len>)` whose
// index came from a COLON finder, with the index VARIABLE's name normalised — the pair is
// written out **10 times in 5 functions across three modules**:
//
//   `emit_base.annObjFieldSplit` · `emit_base.shapeInnerFieldSplit` ·
//   `emit_classify.shapeFieldParse` · `typecheck.nameToTyReal` · `typecheck.canonShapeName`
//
// **THE CENSUS SCORED THIS TWO, AND THE REASON IS A NEW NORMALISATION FAILURE.** Its copies
// table read "2 | `X.slice(colonAt + 1, X.length)` (field TYPE after `:`) | typecheck x2":
// it saw ONE half in ONE module because the other three modules spell the index `ci` where
// the checker spells it `colonAt`. The census abstracts the RECEIVER and (since #1250) the
// LENGTH expression; it does not abstract the INDEX VARIABLE's name. That is the seventh
// distinct normalisation failure this programme has recorded and the second family the
// census never opened.
//
// **EIGHT OF THE TEN ROUTE HERE; THE FAMILY IS EXPLICITLY HALF-ROUTED AT
// `emit_classify.shapeFieldParse`.** That file is held by a concurrent slice, so its two
// writings (the `ci < 0` guard, `part.slice(0, ci)` / `part.slice(ci + 1, part.length)`) are
// FILED, not routed. It already imports this module and already holds the index, so it is a
// two-line substitution for whoever owns the file next — the same shape, and the same reason,
// as W2's four-writing residue in `emit_collect.vl`, which this slice closes.
//
// **THE INDEX IS PASSED IN, AND THE CUT IS DELIMITER-BLIND** — the same ruling `groupInnerOf`,
// `gaeArgsTextOf` and `fnRetTextOf` all took, and here the evidence is FOUR DIFFERENT GUARDS
// in front of ONE operation: `annObjFieldSplit` and `shapeInnerFieldSplit` gate `ci <= 0`,
// `shapeFieldParse` gates `ci < 0`, `nameToTyReal` gates `colonAt > 0`, `canonShapeName`
// gates `colonAt >= 0`. The finders disagree too — three ask `indexOf(":")` (first colon,
// depth-BLIND, over a name their caller has already flattened) and two ask
// `tyTopIndexOf(field, ':', 0, 0)` (first TOP-LEVEL colon). Folding either the guard or the
// finder in would be wrong for the other sites; both stay where they are.
//
// **THE NAME HALF HAS A SIXTH SPELLING THAT KEEPS THE DELIMITER**, and taking the index as an
// ARGUMENT is exactly what lets it route without a second body. `canonShapeName` writes
// `field.slice(0, colonAt + 1)` — the field name WITH its colon, because it is rebuilding the
// shape text and needs the separator back. That is `fieldNameOf(field, colonAt + 1)`, the same
// home at a different index, not a wider one. This is the endpoint-normalisation trap #1250
// recorded costing W1 a site twice; a home that hard-coded `+ 1` inside would have needed a
// second body for the other five writings.
//
// **BOTH BODIES AGREE WITH THE RAW EXPRESSION ON EVERY INPUT, not merely on every reachable
// one, and that is by CONSTRUCTION rather than by argument**: each is the site's own slice with
// the index lifted to a parameter, so there is no bound to discharge. (VL's `.slice` CLAMPS
// rather than traps — re-verified here by running all eight out-of-range endpoint shapes,
// including `"abc".slice(99, 3)` → `""` and `"".slice(1, 0)` → `""` — so even a caller handing
// in an index past the end gets the identical answer from either form.)
//
// **`fieldNameOf` IS `gaeHeadNameOf` AS AN OPERATION, AND THEY STAY TWO NAMES ON PURPOSE.**
// Both are `slice(0, i)`; so, one delimiter over, is `passRowName`'s cut in `emit_sections`.
// This file already carries that redundancy deliberately in the other direction —
// `fnRetTextOf` (`slice(at + 2, len)`), `gaeArgsTextOf` (`slice(lt + 1, len - 1)`) and
// `mapSpellValName` are three near-identical tails under three grammar names — because the
// unit this programme homes is the QUESTION, not the arithmetic. Collapsing them would put
// `gaeHeadNameOf`'s twelve call sites under a name that says nothing about which bracket they
// mean, and the merged body would be reached by two grammars whose guards, finders and
// delimiters have nothing in common. The cost of keeping them apart is one function frame, and
// it is measured in the byte table rather than assumed.
//
// The field NAME of a shape-body field `name:type`, given the colon at `ci` — everything
// BEFORE the index. Hand in `ci + 1` for the spelling that keeps the colon.
// (Bound rather than left as the trailing expression: a bare member-call statement is not a
// lowerable form, the same reason `arrElemNameRaw` binds its slice.)
export function fieldNameOf(field: string, ci: i32) {
  const fname = field.slice(0, ci)
  fname
}
// The field TYPE TEXT of a shape-body field `name:type`, given the colon at `ci` —
// everything past the colon, to the end of the field. The field's own END is the caller's:
// all five sites have already cut the field out of the shape body (at a top-level comma or
// via `gaeSplitArgs`), so the tail runs to `field.length` and NOT to `length - 1` — which is
// the one character that separates this cut from `gaeArgsTextOf`, whose operand still carries
// its closing `>`.
export function fieldTypeTextOf(field: string, ci: i32) {
  const ftext = field.slice(ci + 1, field.length)
  ftext
}

// ── the MAP-NAME grammar: `{[K]:V}` — OPEN, SPAN, and the KEY/VALUE CUT ───────────────
//
// The parser's index-signature spelling. It is not assembled character by character by the
// thing that reads it, it is assembled character by character by the thing that WRITES it:
// `parser.vl:538` is literally `name = "{[" + keyName + "]:" + valName + "}"`, and the KEY
// is a bare `IDENT` token ("The KEY is a bare identifier token, not a parsed type, so it
// stays text on the node"). Every property this family relies on is a property of that one
// concatenation, which is why the grammar can be stated once instead of re-derived.
//
// **THREE COPIES OF IT LIVED IN THIS FILE**, and they did not even agree on how to find the
// key's end: `nameToTyReal`'s map arm and `mapShapeKeyName` scanned for the first `]:` pair,
// while `canonShapeName` ran the compiler's depth walk (`tyGroupEndIndex`) over the shape's
// interior. On a parser-produced name the two agree for the reason above — the key holds no
// bracket, so its `]` is both the first `]` and the depth-0 closer — and this home keeps the
// WALK, so the map grammar stops hand-rolling a scan of its own and asks the compiler's one
// depth home instead. (`mapShapeKeyName` is gone: it WAS `mapSpellKeyName`, character for
// character, and its one caller now says so.)
//
// **THE FOURTH COPY IN `emit_base.vl` IS NOW GONE TOO (D-MAPOPENDOWN).** Its `nameIsMapOpen`
// was this file's `nameIsMapOpen`, line for line, so D-MAPSPELL's own hand-off — recorded here
// as a checklist for whoever next owned that file — has been discharged: the definition is
// deleted and `emit_base.vl` republishes THIS one, which is what makes `emit_classify` and
// `emit_collect` compile untouched. `mapValNameOf` there STAYS: it is `mapSpellValName` with a
// DIFFERENT span rule — it gates on `nameIsMap` (opens `{[`, does not end `[]`) where this one
// gates on a closing `}`, so it ACCEPTS `{[string]:i32}|null` and returns the garbled
// `"i32}|nul"` that its own callers strip for (see `emit_collect.vl:2535`). The two are NOT
// interchangeable and must not be merged by eyeball; its header, which used to cite a
// `typecheck.vl parseMapName` that has not existed for many merges, now says so.
//
// THE ONE HOME of the MAP-NAME OPENER: a `{` at index 0 whose NEXT character is `[`. Over
// the `length >= 2` names that open with `{` this is the exact COMPLEMENT of
// `nameIsShapeOpen` above.
export function nameIsMapOpen(name: string) {
  if name.length < 2 { return false }
  name[0] == '{' && name[1] == '['
}
// THE ONE HOME of the map-name SPAN: the opener AND a final `}`, so the whole name is the
// map and not something carrying one (`{[string]:i32}[]` is a LIST of maps and
// `{[string]:i32}|null` a union with one — both open `{[` and neither ends `}`). A LAYER
// over the opener, not a second copy of it. This is the map twin of
// `emit_base.nameIsShapeSpanEnds`, which cannot serve because it carries the
// `name[1] != '['` conjunct that makes it reject every map name.
export function nameIsMapSpanEnds(name: string) {
  if !nameIsMapOpen(name) { return false }
  name[name.length - 1] == '}'
}
// THE ONE HOME of the KEY CUT's scan: the index of the `]` that closes the KEY of a
// `{[`-opening name, or -1 when `name` does not open a map spelling or its key never closes
// into a `]:` pair.
//
// The walk is `tyGroupEndIndex`, entered at index 2 — the character after the `[` this
// function has just proved is at index 1, which is exactly the `from`-gating contract that
// home documents ("Callers gate on the OPENER themselves"). The `]:` pair is then confirmed
// at the site the walk stopped: the walk answers "where does the key's bracket close", the
// grammar additionally requires a `:` to follow it, and a closer that is not `]` at all
// (reachable only on a name no producer writes) is rejected rather than sliced around.
//
// The `length >= 5` bound the two scanning copies spelled is IMPLIED and dropped: a name
// passing this function has `{`, `[`, a `]` at index >= 2 and a `:` after it, so it is at
// least the 4 characters of `{[]:` — and every consumer that also asks `nameIsMapSpanEnds`
// gets the 5th from the `}`.
export function mapSpellKeyEnd(name: string) {
  if !nameIsMapOpen(name) { return -1 }
  const ke = tyGroupEndIndex(name, 2)
  if ke < 0 { return -1 }
  if ke + 1 >= name.length { return -1 }
  if name[ke] != ']' { return -1 }
  if name[ke + 1] != ':' { return -1 }
  ke
}
// THE ONE HOME of the KEY CUT: the key type-name `K` of a `{[K]:V}` spelling — the text
// between the `{[` and the key's closing `]` — or "" when `name` does not open one. Used to
// give an actionable diagnostic for an unsupported (non-`string`) map key instead of a bare
// "unknown type '{[i32]:i32}'", which is why it deliberately does NOT ask for the closing
// `}`: a LIST of i32-keyed maps (`{[i32]:i32}[]`) is the same user mistake and gets the same
// message.
export function mapSpellKeyName(name: string) {
  const ke = mapSpellKeyEnd(name)
  if ke < 0 { return "" }
  const k = name.slice(2, ke)
  k
}
// THE ONE HOME of the VALUE CUT: the value type-name `V` of a `{[K]:V}` spelling — the text
// after the `]:` and before the closing `}` — or "" when `name` is not a WHOLE map spelling.
// Unlike the key cut this one takes the SPAN, because the closing `}` is the value's right
// edge: without it there is no value text to cut, only a suffix of a bigger name.
export function mapSpellValName(name: string) {
  if !nameIsMapSpanEnds(name) { return "" }
  const ke = mapSpellKeyEnd(name)
  if ke < 0 { return "" }
  const v = name.slice(ke + 2, name.length - 1)
  v
}

// ── the GENERIC-APPLICATION grammar: `gaeLtAt` FINDS the `<`, `annGenAppSpanEnds` SPANS ──
//
// **BOTH HOMES MOVED DOWN HERE FROM `emit_base.vl` (D-GENAPPDOWN)**, the same inversion the
// PAREN / ARRAY / SHAPE / BRACE grammars took and for the same reason: the CHECKER asks both
// questions, `emit_base.vl` imports THIS module, and a route upward would cycle the module
// graph. `emit_base.vl` keeps publishing both names, so `emit_collect`'s four call sites and
// `emit_classify`'s two compile untouched.
//
// The spelling is the parser's: `parseTypeName` folds a generic's arguments into the declared
// name, so a DECLARATION is `Box<T>` / `Pair<A,B>` and an APPLICATION is `Box<i32>` — one
// concatenation, and every property this family relies on is a property of it. The head is an
// identifier run, so the FIRST `<` is the head's right edge; the argument list is the rest,
// and it closes the name.
//
// **THE `<`-SCAN AND THE SPAN ARE TWO PREDICATES, NOT ONE, AND THIS FILE ASKED FOR BOTH
// SEPARATELY.** Censused structurally over the `'<'` / `'>'` character literals (13 and 12
// tree-wide), this file carried the `<`-scan written out as a hand-rolled `let/while/flag`
// loop THREE times — `nameToTyReal`'s application arm, `nameIsGenAppOfDecl`, and pass 0a's
// generic-declaration registration — and TWO of those three wrapped it in the full span
// triple (`length >= 3`, a final `>`, `lt > 0`). Pass 0a is the site that proves the split is
// real: it registers a DECLARATION, where the `<` is all it wants and the trailing `>` is
// re-read later by the parameter-list cut, so routing it to the span would have been a
// tightening, not a dedup.
//
// THE ONE HOME of the `<`-scan: the position of the first `<` in `name`, or -1. A positive
// result marks a generic declaration or application name; index 0 does not (a name whose
// FIRST character is `<` has no head, so every caller gates on `> 0`, never `>= 0`).
export function gaeLtAt(name: string) {
  let i = 0
  while i < name.length {
    if name[i] == '<' { return i }
    i = i + 1
  }
  -1
}
// THE ONE HOME of the GENERIC-APPLICATION SPAN test: a name whose FINAL character is `>` and
// which has a `<` at a positive index — `Box<i32>`, `Pair<K, V>`. The TEST half of the
// `Head<…>` grammar, split out from `emit_base.annGenAppDecompose` because callers want only
// the boolean and their own cut, not the split argument list.
//
// The `>` compare stays in FRONT of `gaeLtAt`'s scan, exactly as the inline spellings had it:
// running a whole-name scan on every annotation that plainly does not end in `>` would be a
// work regression. `gaeLtAt(ann) > 0` implies `ann.length >= 2`, so the final-character read
// below is in bounds for every name that reaches it, and the `n < 3` cheap reject is kept as
// the fast path it always was — `lt > 0` plus a trailing `>` already forces `n >= 3`, so it
// decides nothing.
//
// The two routed sites in this file re-ask `gaeLtAt` for their own head/argument slices
// rather than threading the index out of the test. That is the trade `annGenAppDecompose` and
// `emit_classify.gaeEnsure` already take, and it costs a second scan only on a name that
// already ended in `>` — every other name is rejected by the character compare before either
// scan runs.
export function annGenAppSpanEnds(ann: string) {
  const n = ann.length
  if n < 3 { return false }
  if ann[n - 1] != '>' { return false }
  gaeLtAt(ann) > 0
}
// ── the GENERIC-APPLICATION CUT: the fourth "test half homed, cut half not" ────────────
//
// `gaeLtAt` FINDS the `<` and `annGenAppSpanEnds` TESTS the span; until here nothing owned
// the two SLICES every one of their callers takes next. Re-censused structurally at
// `17eb646` — every `.slice(0, <var>)` and every `.slice(<var> + 1, <len> - 1)` tree-wide,
// classified by whether the index came from a `<`-scan — the pair is written out **16
// times in 9 functions** (HEAD 9, ARGUMENT TEXT 7):
//
//   HEAD  `driver.modGenBase` · `emit_base.annGenAppDecompose` · `emit_classify.gaeEnsure` ·
//         `typecheck.nameToTyReal` / `nameIsGenAppOfDecl` / `canonEmitNameAt` /
//         `checkProgramNode` · `emit_collect.gaeCollectDecls` / `gaeInternAppFieldShapes`
//   ARGS  the same, minus `modGenBase` and `nameIsGenAppOfDecl` (neither wants the list)
//
// **ALL SIXTEEN NOW ROUTE. THE FOUR `emit_collect.vl` WRITINGS THAT WERE FILED AS A HELD-FILE
// RESIDUE ARE CLOSED**, and closing them settled a number the census had wrong twice over: it
// listed that file at ONE writing, W3's re-census raised it to two functions and FOUR, and the
// resolver confirms four. `gaeInternAppFieldShapes` was never on the census's list at all —
// `gaeCollectDecls` was, and only its HEAD half. Nothing else in the file is this operation:
// its whole `.slice` population is those four. THE HAND-WRITTEN COPY COUNT FOR THIS GRAMMAR IS
// ZERO, TREE-WIDE.
//
// **THE INDEX IS PASSED IN, NOT RE-SCANNED, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE SHAPE OF THIS CUT.** Every
// routed site already holds the `<` position in a local when it takes its slice — three of
// them (`annGenAppDecompose`, `gaeEnsure`, `nameToTyReal`) precisely because `gaeLtAt` was
// re-asked after `annGenAppSpanEnds` returned. A home that re-scanned would add a third walk
// of the same name to every one of them; a home that took the index adds nothing at all.
//
// **DELIMITER-BLIND, LIKE `groupInnerOf`, AND FOR THE SAME MEASURED REASON.** The routed
// sites do not agree on the guard in front of the cut: five ask `annGenAppSpanEnds`, one
// (`modGenBase`) asks only `lt >= 0` and MUST fall through to the whole name when there is
// no `<`, and one (`checkProgramNode`) asks `gaeLtAt(...) > 0` on a DECLARATION where no
// trailing `>` has been established yet. Folding any of those three into the cut would be
// wrong for the other two, so the guard stays at the call site — the same ruling W1 took at
// ten sites, twenty-two sites and now here.
//
// The HEAD of a generic application/declaration name `Head<A,B>` given the `<` at `lt`.
// (Bound rather than left as the trailing expression: a bare member-call statement is not a
// lowerable form, the same reason `arrElemNameRaw` binds its slice.)
export function gaeHeadNameOf(name: string, lt: i32) {
  const head = name.slice(0, lt)
  head
}
// The ARGUMENT TEXT of a generic application/declaration name `Head<A,B>` — everything
// between the `<` at `lt` and the name's LAST character. The callers split it themselves
// (`gaeSplitArgs`, `splitTypeName`, `splitGenArgs`) or store it raw (`gaeCollectDecls`), and
// they do not agree on which, which is why the SPLIT is not folded in here.
//
// The closing `>` is taken positionally — `name.length - 1` — exactly as all seven writings
// spelled it, including the two that spelled the length as a hoisted local (`emit_base`'s
// `n`, `typecheck.nameToTyReal`'s `nlen`). Those two are the LENGTH-EXPRESSION normalisation
// failure #1250 recorded costing W1 a site twice; this census normalised for it up front.
export function gaeArgsTextOf(name: string, lt: i32) {
  const args = name.slice(lt + 1, name.length - 1)
  args
}
